                <pb facs="0210" n="176" sameAs="frus:frus1870/pg176"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">No. 126.</note>
                <head>Mr. Motley to Mr. Fish.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 486.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United
                                States,</hi><hi rendition="#italic"> London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1870-10-13"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >October</hi> 13, 1870</date>. (Received October
                    26.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I sent you on the 10th instant a dispatch,
                    partly in cipher, of which the text is as follows. It reached me from Mr.
                    Washburne by what is now called balloon post:</p>
                <pb facs="0211" n="177" xml:id="pg177"/>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">From, Motley</hi>, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">London, to Fish, Secretary of State, Washington
                        City.</hi></p>
                <p>I received the following in a letter this morning; it is dated September 30:</p>
                <p>“Telegraph Washington that Paris has five hundred thousand troops. Best spirit
                    prevails. People united. Government strong. Desperate resistance.”</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>JOHN LOTHROP MOTLEY.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
